---
import '../styles/orientation-landing.css';
type Kind = 'workbench' | 'library' | 'atlas';
const { kind } = Astro.props as { kind: Kind };
const pages = {
  workbench: { title:'The Workbench', hero:'Where ideas become useful, inspectable work.', lede:'The Workbench is Hearth & Code in motion: small systems, research procedures, and agent-assisted workflows designed to produce a concrete next step.', intro:'A project earns its place here by doing a bounded job. It may be unfinished, experimental, or being reconsidered, but it should be clear about what it is for, what it has shown, and what remains open.', sections:[['A practical loop','Orient the task. Bind sources and boundaries. Make the smallest useful change. Check what happened. Leave a short handoff. This sequence turns broad ambition into work another person, or a future version of yourself, can actually resume.'],['A worked pattern: the reviewable brief','Start with a question, then name the decision it supports, the available sources, the paths that must not change, and the condition that ends the work. An agent can draft and compare inside that envelope; it cannot silently enlarge it.'],['Agentic engineering in practice','Agents are most useful when each receives a bounded objective, relevant context, allowed tools, a verification method, and a clear handoff. The result is not an impressive transcript; it is an artifact whose origin and limits are easy to see.'],['What a prototype proves','A prototype can show that one task is feasible or that an interface has a useful shape. It does not prove an entire product, system architecture, or future outcome. Keeping that distinction visible lets experiments stay honest and productive.']], prompt:'Turn this research question into a bounded work brief. Return: (1) the decision it could inform, (2) sources needed, (3) assumptions to test, (4) a smallest demonstrable artifact, (5) verification, and (6) a next-step handoff. Do not claim facts without a source.' },
  library: { title:'The Library', hero:'A shelf of working knowledge, kept in a shape people can use.', lede:'The Library is a reference-first memory for the practice: sources, notes, evidence, proposals, and published work that can be traced, revisited, and built upon.', intro:'A library is not a content dump. It is a way to show the shape of a body of work without asking a reader to absorb every ledger or internal process record.', sections:[['Seven useful states','Source names where something came from. Evidence records what was observed. Inference explains a conclusion. Hypothesis marks a testable possibility. Proposal suggests a move. Draft is unfinished language. Published is a released public record.'],['Primitives before complexity','A primitive is a small, named thing that can be combined without losing its meaning: a source, a claim, a boundary, a task, a receipt, a decision, a return link. Good systems make these parts visible before they become elaborate.'],['Operational intelligence','Operational intelligence is the practice of making work answerable: distinguish observation from interpretation, place a limit next to a claim, and preserve enough context for correction or resumption. It is practical information architecture, not a claim that software is autonomous.'],['A shelf, not a vault','Research notes, syntheses, prototypes, journal articles, and talks can appear here in summary form. Private reflection, inbound material, account details, and unreviewed operational records stay out of the public shelf by default.']], prompt:'Read the material below and produce a small library entry. Separate source facts, observed evidence, inferences, hypotheses, and proposals. Include a two-sentence summary, what would change the conclusion, and the next record that should exist.' },
  atlas: { title:'The Atlas', hero:'Techniques worth borrowing, with the conditions that make them work.', lede:'The Atlas collects reusable ways of thinking and building. Each technique is defined plainly, demonstrated in miniature, and paired with the failure mode that keeps it honest.', intro:'Thinking comes first, technique comes second, mapping comes third. A visitor should be able to take one practice away and try it in their own work without inheriting Hearth & Code’s private scaffolding.', sections:[['Bound the question','A good prompt begins with a decision and a boundary. Instead of asking for everything about a topic, ask what evidence would change a specific choice, which sources count, and when the inquiry should stop.'],['Climb the provenance ladder','Keep the path from claim to source visible: direct source, observation, interpretation, and proposed action are different rungs. If a rung is missing, say so. This makes an answer easier to challenge without making the whole effort collapse.'],['Compose, then delegate','Split an ambitious task into small units with defined inputs and outputs. Ask an agent to produce candidate plans or comparisons, then use the resulting artifacts to decide the next unit. Recursive prompting works when each recursion has a stopping point.'],['Use power-of-two rhythm carefully','Groups of four or eight can make a complex surface easier to scan and compare. They are a compositional aid, not evidence that a domain has exactly four or eight truths. Let the material set the count when it needs to.']], prompt:'Design a four-step inquiry for the decision below. For each step name the input, method, output, failure condition, and what the next step may rely on. Keep the steps small enough to review independently.' }
} as const;
const page = pages[kind];
const next = kind === 'workbench' ? ['/library/','Library'] : kind === 'library' ? ['/atlas/','Atlas'] : ['/','Orientation'];
const demonstrations = {
  workbench: { title: 'Demonstration: turn a vague request into a reviewable build', situation: 'A founder asks for “an AI research assistant that knows our product.” The useful first move is not to build a chat interface. It is to identify the decision the assistant should support, the documents it may use, the material it must not expose, and one answer that can be checked by a person.', steps: ['Name the decision: “Which onboarding gap should we address next?”', 'Collect only the approved product notes, support themes, and current metrics.', 'Ask an agent for a comparison with quotations or source locators, not a recommendation dressed as fact.', 'Have a person select the next experiment and record why other options were deferred.'], output: 'The output is a compact decision brief: question, sources, candidate answers, caveats, chosen action, and a re-entry note. That is demoable work. A chat transcript alone is not.' },
  library: { title: 'Demonstration: make one claim easier to inspect', situation: 'A page says a workflow “improves handoff.” That sentence means little until the record distinguishes what was observed from what is inferred and what remains a proposal.', steps: ['Source: a task brief and the handoff it produced.', 'Evidence: the next worker could identify the changed files and verification without asking for reconstruction.', 'Inference: structured handoffs reduce some re-entry cost in this setting.', 'Proposal: use the same record shape for the next comparable task and check whether it still helps.'], output: 'The reader gets a small knowledge unit instead of a grand claim. If later evidence disagrees, the inference changes while the source and observation remain intact.' },
  atlas: { title: 'Demonstration: a recursive prompt sequence', situation: 'Suppose a team needs to decide whether to introduce a new agent workflow. One large prompt invites vague advice. A four-pass sequence produces reviewable parts.', steps: ['Frame: define the decision, audience, constraints, and stop condition.', 'Map: ask for the smallest set of source classes and missing evidence.', 'Compare: ask for two viable approaches, their tradeoffs, and a falsifier for each.', 'Commit: produce the smallest experiment, its owner, test, and return condition.'], output: 'This sequence is useful because each pass narrows the next. If a source is missing or the decision changes, return to the relevant pass rather than asking the model to “think harder.”' }
} as const;
const demo = demonstrations[kind];
---
<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8" /><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" /><meta name="description" content={page.lede} /><meta name="theme-color" content="#1c1714" /><title>{page.title} | Hearth &amp; Code</title></head><body class="orientation-body"><div class="orientation-atmosphere" aria-hidden="true"></div><a class="orientation-skip" href="#main-content">Skip to content</a><header class="orientation-header"><a class="orientation-wordmark" href="/">Hearth <em>&amp;</em> Code</a><p>{page.title}</p><a class="orientation-journal" href="https://blog.hearthandcode.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Field Journal ↗</a></header><main id="main-content"><section class="builder-hero"><p class="orientation-kicker">{kind} / practical systems</p><h1>{page.hero}</h1><p class="orientation-lede">{page.lede}</p></section><section class="builder-introduction"><div><p class="orientation-kicker">The purpose</p><h2>A deeper reading layer for the practice.</h2></div><div class="builder-reading"><p>{page.intro}</p><p>Read it as an invitation to inspect the method, take what is useful, and adapt it to the real conditions of your own work.</p></div></section><section class="builder-charter"><div class="orientation-section-heading"><p class="orientation-kicker">Four working areas</p><h2>Clear enough to use tomorrow.</h2></div><ol class="builder-dimension-grid">{page.sections.map(([name, text], index) => <li><span>{String(index+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><h3>{name}</h3><p>{text}</p></li>)}</ol></section><section class="deep-demo"><p class="orientation-kicker">Worked example</p><h2>{demo.title}</h2><p class="deep-demo-lede">{demo.situation}</p><ol>{demo.steps.map((step, index) => <li><span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span><p>{step}</p></li>)}</ol><p class="deep-demo-output"><strong>What this produces:</strong> {demo.output}</p></section><section class="builder-principle"><div><p class="orientation-kicker">Try the pattern</p><h2>A prompt is a work instruction, not a wish.</h2></div><pre class="deep-prompt"><code>{page.prompt}</code></pre></section><section class="deep-notes"><p class="orientation-kicker">Practice note</p><p>These examples show public-facing method patterns. They are not a substitute for judgment, direct source review, or the local constraints of a real project. A useful method is one that reveals its assumptions, gives a reviewer something concrete to check, and leaves a way to revise the work after new evidence appears.</p></section><nav class="builder-next" aria-label="Continue through Hearth and Code"><a href="/">← Back to Orientation</a><a href={next[0]}>Continue to {next[1]} →</a></nav></main><footer class="orientation-footer"><p>Hearth &amp; Code</p><a href="/builder/">Meet the Builder →</a></footer></body></html>
